From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 16:26:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/26] fix(hooks): enforce the patches/ export on every branch
 push (gcp-2qk)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

patches/ is a derived artifact — the `git format-patch BASELINE..HEAD` export of
the fork's divergence — so every fork commit has to be re-exported. The
obligation lands on whoever authors the commit, but .githooks/pre-push only
validated pushes whose remote ref was refs/heads/integration:

    if [ "$remote_ref" != "refs/heads/integration" ]; then continue; fi

The author of a fork commit pushes a topic branch. The integration push is
performed later, by the refinery. So the only guarded push was the one the
author never performs: a stale export went out on a green local run and
surfaced a full agent cycle later at the merge gate, on a branch that had
already been reviewed. The refinery measured 3 of 4 open polecat branches
unlandable for exactly this reason, with the fourth proving the step is
discoverable — a missing enforcement point, not a forgetful author.

The hook now validates every pushed branch ref and passes the SHA git is about
to send to check-patches as REV, so what gets validated is what lands. Tag
pushes and deletions are skipped (no tree to validate), as are branches that do
not descend from BASELINE — an upstream-only branch carries no fork divergence,
and BASELINE..HEAD there would describe an unrelated stack the author cannot
act on.

check-patches moves out of the Makefile into scripts/check-patches.sh, matching
how `make upgrade` already delegates to scripts/upgrade-integration.sh. Beyond
readability that buys the REV parameter, and one behavioural change: patches/ is
now read out of REV's tree via git cat-file rather than off disk. A push sends
commits, not the working tree, so an export regenerated but left uncommitted is
not what lands — counting it would admit exactly the drift being guarded
against. `make check-patches` still defaults to REV=HEAD.

Validation: tests/test_fork_patch_guard.sh drives the real hook end-to-end
through a scratch repo with a bare remote — the guard only ever runs inside
`git push`, so no existing test sweep touches it. Verified red against the
previous ref-filtered hook (both stale-topic-branch cases accepted the push,
"hook did not fire") and green against this one, 7/7. gofumpt/go vet/go build/
go test -race, golangci-lint (0 issues), validate_registry.py, pytest tests
(101 passed) and shellcheck over the three shell files are all clean; the 12
gofumpt findings are pre-existing on integration and unchanged by this diff,
which touches no Go.

Not done here: the bead's alternative of running check-patches in CI on
pull_request. ci.yml triggers only on main and is upstream-tracked, so
fork-specific jobs there would grow the patch stack's conflict surface at every
baseline upgrade without adding enforcement the hook does not already provide
earlier. Worth revisiting if the fork ever gains its own workflow file.
